[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Microsoft Surface Book logo on a large screen at a presentation.
AUDIO: 'Let\'s go back in time 10 years to 2016. Apple was flying high. They had spent the previous decade proving that by controlling both the hardware and the software, it let them make some amazing computers. And Microsoft was disappointed in the lack of creativity'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they were seeing from some of their hardware partners who they relied on to make the computers that their operating system, Windows, ran on. So, Microsoft found their own Johnny IV, Panos Pane, and tasked him with leading up the'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Surface hardware division. The mission to make great hardware, hardware that pushed the boundaries of what a Windows PC could be. Get creative, think out of the side of the box, go wild. And wild'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they did. They experimented with new form factors like the hybrid tablet surface devices. They experimented with material like let\'s put Alcantara fabric on laptops. Let\'s create a crazy hinge'

[00:52] [scene:talking_head]
VISUAL: The reviewer is speaking directly to the camera.
AUDIO: 'for a laptop screen. What if an Android phone had two screens? Let\'s try it. The Surface Duo phone is still one of my favorite pieces of hardware I\'ve ever owned. And and all of these devices work with a pen, the Surface Pen. And for me,'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a creative pro who had dabbled in illustration for years, this was enough for me to set my MacBook aside and actually try Windows. But it got better. In October of 2016, Penos pane got up on'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stage and unveiled this, the Surface Studio. This was an artist\'s dream. It was Microsoft\'s answer to the Apple iMac. An all-in-one computer with a beautiful 28 in display, an amazingly'

[01:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engineered stand that let you smoothly pull it up and pull your monitor down into this like perfect drawing position. This was it. One of Microsoft\'s main goals with the Surface line was to test'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the market with different products to see what consumers responded to and then let their hardware partners iterate on it. partners like Lenovo, Dell, Asus, they could run with these designs and make cheaper alternatives that customers'

[01:58] [scene:talking_head]
VISUAL: The reviewer is speaking directly to the camera.
AUDIO: 'would then buy. Better hardware would push them to make better software. Better software would then enable better hardware. Now, the Surface Pro tablets, a decent success. We saw a bunch of clones. To this day, we still see clones'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of that. Microsoft\'s attempts to make Windows a seamless two screen operating system, a little bit more of a rocky road there. And the Surface Studio, despite making me and many of the people watching this channel way back then'

[02:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'salivate, just didn\'t make the marketplace impact that Microsoft had hoped, it was a flawed product. First of all, it was underpowered out of the gate. To compound it, it wasn\'t upgradable. And what really killed it'

[02:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was that it was super super expensive. So expensive that I couldn\'t afford one at the time. So I never actually reviewed the studio. This was a much smaller channel back then. And although'

[02:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the design was great, nobody wanted to spend that much money on a computer with so little power. Especially since the Apple iMacs at the time were half the price for twice as much compute. Back in 2016, what I was hoping would happen is'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that other Windows PC manufacturers would pick up the torch and make cheaper, more powerful clones that we could all enjoy. But alas, that never really happened. Lenovo took a shot at it. There was a Kickstarter a few years'

[03:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ago that literally made a one:1 clone of the Surface Studio by a Chinese company that has disappeared from the internet since. But now, the monitor company ALogic has a really cool spin on this form factor that solves a lot of the'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Surface Studios problems while hanging on to what made it so cool in the first place. That stand that this was the longest intro to a video I think I\'ve ever done. We don\'t we don\'t need the'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'theme song. You guys could stop playing the theme song. This is Brad. He likes to review the tech. He also likes to draw. And this here is his theme song. Well, I guess the theme song really will'

