VISUAL: A person interacts with a webpage on the ALOGIC monitor using touch gestures.
AUDIO: 'mid. I think the Mac Mini speakers are actually actually sound better in this case. My MacBook speakers definitely sound better. So, if you\'ve ever tried a touchscreen on a Mac, it\'s it\'s not the best experience. Touchscreens on Windows. It\'s just Windows is designed'

[14:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to be used for touch. So, it works so much better there. I think they realized this that that touch on Max kind of stinks. So, they made some of their own software that lets you create your own hand gestures and that sort of thing to use it. Now, we are getting some'

[14:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'touchscreen max this fall. That\'s what the rumor mill out there says. So, it\'s possible that it\'s going to be really easy to integrate that with these kind of third party touchcreens in the future. We will see. I\'m hopeful. But on a Mac, not all the gestures are going to'

[14:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'work the way you would expect them to. like if you were using an iPhone or an iPad. Okay, so we we got to get here to the pen. This is an MPPP pen, and I\'m not surprised that that\'s what they\'re using here. This is the same exact pen'

[14:57] [scene:talking_head]
VISUAL: The reviewer is speaking directly to the camera.
AUDIO: 'that they\'re using on the Surface Pro devices, that Surface Studio I talked about at the beginning of the video. Alogic has their own version of the stylus, and you can pick that up for $120, but this is the same underlying tech. Um, that that stylus came to me'

[15:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about a week and a half after they sent me the monitor. So, a lot of the drawing I did, I was using a Microsoft Surface stylus. It\'s the same thing. Their stylus does have a USB type-C plug, so you can plug it in and and charge it up'

[15:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that way. Microsoft\'s made it really easy for manufacturers to add this kind of functionality to their devices without a ton of hassle. And unlike a Wacom tablet or a Huion tablet or something like that, you don\'t have to go through the process of installing'

[15:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drivers or anything like that to make it work. It\'s just plugandplay. You grab a pen and you start drawing. So, the pros and the cons of an MPP stylus like this. The pros is that it works on a lot of'

[15:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different devices. It\'s easy for the manufacturer to implement. It tends to keep the price down. Although at $120, this is a very expensive stylus. These styluses are designed for handwriting. Like like that is their superpower for'

[15:58] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A hand draws on the ALOGIC monitor, showing line work in a drawing application.
AUDIO: 'taking notes. Not so much for drawing. So why is that? Well, one, the palm rejection is really, really good, which is something that you need when you\'re doing handwriting. It\'s really nice to have for drawing, too. But for longer lines, for angled lines, there\'s a lot'

[16:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of heavily pronounced digital wave that you\'re going to find to pens like this. This isn\'t something you\'re going to notice when you\'re writing fast and taking notes and and just making letter forms, but when you get those longer'

[16:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lines, when you\'re getting loopy lines, that\'s where you\'re going to start to see that wave appear. It\'s definitely something you\'re going to notice when you\'re drawing. And my art style especially, uh, is pretty dependent on like crisp ink lines. So, I definitely see it right away when I start using a'

[16:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pen like this. If you have a more painterly style where you aren\'t as dependent on like really crisp line work, you know, it\'s probably going to matter a lot less to you. Pressure on the pen was kind of a bummer here, too. That\'s something that these pens usually'

[16:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do fairly well. Even though there are 4,000 levels of pressure sensitivity, which is more than enough, it doesn\'t activate evenly, and it creates this stairstepping effect or this shoestringing effect at the end of lines. It\'s especially noticeable on'

[17:02] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A hand draws a large loop on the ALOGIC monitor with a stylus, demonstrating pen performance.
AUDIO: 'fast lines like hatching lines. But you are also going to see it in your just everyday line drawing, too. What about this pen on Macs? Well, I went to the website that is listed in the'

[17:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instructions that came with the pen itself to grab the drivers. That link did not work. I I tried it on several different browsers. I went on Windows just to make sure that it wasn\'t just happening on a Mac. No drivers there. So, I Googled it and I found the drivers'

[17:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that way. It installs the touch interface stuff as well as the pen drivers. And the pen is it is it\'s really bad on Macs. I\'m not surprised. Uh it\'s really unusable because of all'

[17:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the jitter. It\'s really bad. So for the pen, I would just totally skip it for the Mac. Not worth it. Uh again, I\'m not surprised. I\'ve seen MPP pens before trying to work on Macs and they never'

[17:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'work well. This one is just really bad. Just skip it. So do I recommend this product? Oh man, I I I wanted to love this. I wanted to love this and I love'

[18:05] [scene:talking_head]
VISUAL: The reviewer is speaking directly to the camera.
AUDIO: 'the stand. Uh I love the size. It just let me down in other areas. The pen is exactly what I expected. I wasn\'t really actually let down with that. It was really the screen and the display'

[18:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'itself. That light bleed along the bottom. If I was using this on a Mac, I think that would drive me nuts. On PC, it didn\'t really bother me too much. Some of those little details, if I was spending my own money on it, I would probably go with a stand that is silver.'

[18:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Although you can\'t get the widescreen with that one. Uh I think if I was spending my own money, I would want to get a higher resolution display. Ultimately comes down to like what do you really really want? Do you really really want this stand? Do you really'

[18:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really want this ultra wide monitor? There\'s nothing else quite like it in terms of form factor out there. Alogic has something unique. And when you have something unique that no one else is really offering, I you can get away with charging a premium for it. If you\'re'

[18:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to use this for notetaking and photo editing, even if you\'re using the pen for like 3D programs, uh I think this display with this arm, it could make sense for you. If you\'re a professional illustrator and you want a monitor that you can draw on most of the'

[19:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'day, all day, every day, I would pass on this one. I I I think that pen is going to hold you back. And I think you could pick up a really really nice $1,500 XP Pen 27in display um and just get a'

